 The sweep is Gauss-Seidel: within a direction each element reads inflow from
 neighbours already updated in the same pass, which is what makes it an exact
 solve for that direction.
 
+## How the solve works
+
+Because the sweep is exact, the distribution that comes out of it depends on
+the one that went in *only* through the moments the scattering source is built
+from -- `Ts`, `qx`, `qy` per element DOF, plus the emission of any diffusely
+reflecting wall.  Call that stack `z`; one outer iteration is then an affine
+map `z -> G z + g`, and the answer is the solution of
+
+    (I - G) z = g
+
+The Fortran's loop is Richardson iteration on that system.  Its contraction
+factor is `1 - O((tau_C Vg / L)^2)`, so in the optically thick cases it needs
+of order `10^6` sweeps -- and long before that it starts reporting a step size
+below `tol` while still being most of the way from its own fixed point.  It is
+kept, verbatim, as `Solver.run_source_iteration`.
+
+`Solver.run` solves the system instead.  GMRES on `(I - G) z = g`, one
+transport sweep per matrix-vector product, converging on the residual of the
+real system rather than on the size of a step.  Optically thick cases get a
+preconditioner: `pybte.accel` writes `I - G` out as a dense matrix by pushing
+every unit source through the sweep at once, on a *reduced* angular quadrature
+chosen so that its second moment -- and so its diffusion limit -- is exact.
+The reduced operator is a few percent of the cost of the fine one and, since
+it only preconditions, it sets the iteration count and never the answer.
+
+All ten shipped cases converge in 11 to 43 sweeps this way, to a transport
+residual of `1e-13` or better.
+
+`performance.scheme` picks between `krylov` (the default), `krylov-plain`
+(never preconditioned) and `source` (the Fortran's loop).
+
